prompt:
Read all canonical Stock Research Lab documents and prior audit/experiment
results first.

Review especially:

- docs/historical-research-protocol.md
- docs/top30-observation-contract.md
- docs/audits/JOB-0006-point-in-time-leakage-audit.md
- docs/experiments/JOB-0007-historical-fixture.md
- docs/experiments/JOB-0008-genuine-telegram-source.md
- docs/migration/legacy-inventory.md

TARGET REPOSITORY

D:/Project/research

LEGACY CODE REFERENCE

D:/Project/stock-trading-system-reference

Treat as strictly READ ONLY.

LEGACY LOCAL DATA REPOSITORY

D:/Project/stock

Treat the entire legacy local repository/data tree as strictly READ ONLY.

Do not modify, rename, normalize, move, delete, re-download, or regenerate
any legacy artifact.

Do not run Git clean/reset/checkout/pull against the legacy repositories.

Do not use GitHub web requests.

IMPORTANT CONTEXT

Existing local assets appear to include historical Telegram Top30 files under
a structure similar to:

D:/Project/stock/data/telegram/daily

with filenames such as:

2023-09-01_07-36-30_15617.txt

There are also existing manifest/data structures and historical Kiwoom/KRX
data in the legacy project.

A correction registry was previously used during Top30 parsing.

Locate the authoritative local correction registry used by the legacy project
rather than inventing or recreating corrections.

Treat all corrections as a separate retrospective correction layer unless
their historical availability can independently be proven.

Do NOT rewrite raw artifacts using correction information.

OBJECTIVE

Determine whether existing local Telegram Top30 artifacts can serve as genuine
historical source evidence for the new point-in-time research pipeline.

This is an AUDIT / INVENTORY job.

Do NOT migrate the full dataset.
Do NOT perform future-price/outcome joins.
Do NOT run the three-year study.
Do NOT acquire new Telegram messages unless explicitly required by a later job.

AUDIT SCOPE

Inspect a SMALL representative sample first.

At minimum select:

- one early historical artifact from the available period;
- one artifact associated with a known correction if identifiable;
- one duplicate/multi-report case if identifiable;
- relevant manifest entries for those artifacts.

Do not scan or parse every file unless inexpensive metadata enumeration is
required to understand the dataset structure.

PART 1 — DATASET INVENTORY

Determine:

- earliest and latest Telegram artifact dates;
- approximate artifact/file count;
- directory organization;
- filename convention;
- message ID representation;
- presence of manifests;
- presence of digests;
- presence of source timestamps;
- presence of ingestion timestamps;
- presence of edit timestamps;
- presence of channel/chat identifiers;
- presence of parser/acquisition version information;
- presence of correction registries.

Distinguish facts found in stored metadata from information inferred only from
filenames.

PART 2 — ARTIFACT PROVENANCE

For each representative artifact determine, where evidence exists:

- exact local path;
- byte length;
- SHA-256 of current local bytes;
- Telegram message ID;
- source/message timestamp;
- edit timestamp;
- ingestion/acquisition timestamp;
- channel/source identity;
- manifest linkage;
- stored digest linkage;
- acquisition implementation that created it;
- whether current bytes can be tied to the stored digest;
- whether historical availability at the intended cutoff is proven,
  plausible-but-unverified, unknown, or contradicted.

Do not invent missing metadata.

PART 3 — ACQUISITION CHAIN

Inspect legacy code to reconstruct the chain that created these files.

Determine as far as evidence permits:

Telegram
    ->
acquisition code
    ->
raw/local file
    ->
manifest
    ->
parser
    ->
processed Top30 data

Identify concrete source files/functions responsible for each step.

Determine whether acquisition preserved:

- original message text;
- Telegram message date;
- edit date;
- message ID;
- collection time;
- raw bytes or normalized text;
- digest at acquisition time.

PART 4 — BYTE / DIGEST INTEGRITY

Where a legacy manifest/digest exists:

- recompute SHA-256 without modifying the source;
- compare with stored digest;
- report match/mismatch.

Where no historical digest exists:

- current SHA-256 may be calculated for future integrity,
  but clearly label it as CURRENT AUDIT DIGEST rather than historical proof.

Never claim a newly calculated digest proves historical bytes.

PART 5 — CORRECTION LAYER

Locate and inspect the legacy Top30 correction registry.

Classify correction types such as:

- report-date correction;
- duplicate/exclusion;
- stock-name correction;
- parsing correction;
- other manual correction.

For representative correction entries determine:

- message ID linkage;
- correction reason;
- whether correction changes raw source interpretation;
- whether correction is retrospective;
- whether its availability at historical cutoff is known.

Architectural rule:

RAW SOURCE MUST REMAIN IMMUTABLE.

Corrections must be represented conceptually as:

raw source
    +
separate correction/enrichment layer

Never rewrite raw Telegram evidence to incorporate a correction.

PART 6 — POINT-IN-TIME CLASSIFICATION

Classify each representative artifact into exactly one:

A — CUTOFF_VERIFIED
Strong evidence that the artifact content and availability are valid for the
historical cutoff.

B — SOURCE_VERIFIED_CONTENT_UNCERTAIN
Telegram/source identity and historical timestamp are supported, but exact
cutoff-visible bytes cannot be proven.

C — LOCALLY_PRESERVED_PROVENANCE_LIMITED
A genuine-looking legacy local artifact exists, but historical availability
or acquisition provenance is insufficient.

D — RETROSPECTIVE_ONLY
Artifact/enrichment is useful only retrospectively.

E — INVALID_OR_INCONSISTENT
Evidence conflicts or integrity validation fails.

Use conservative classification.

PART 7 — COMPATIBILITY WITH NEW PIPELINE

Determine whether representative legacy artifacts can be adapted into:

immutable fixture manifest
    ->
Top30Observation
    ->
HistoricalIdentityBundle
    ->
AssessmentPayload
    ->
write-once frozen assessment

without weakening JOB-0006 leakage protections.

Do NOT force conversion if provenance is insufficient.

PART 8 — EXISTING KRX/KIWOOM DATA

Inventory only enough to determine whether the legacy local repository already
contains useful historical daily market data that may later support outcome
evaluation.

Do NOT join it to Top30 assessments in this job.

Report:

- what types of KRX/Kiwoom daily data appear to exist;
- date coverage if cheaply determinable;
- whether provenance/manifests exist;
- whether these should be audited separately before outcome use.

Do not perform performance calculations.

SECURITY

Do not read or print secret values.

Do not inspect .env contents.

Do not print Telegram session contents.

Session files may be identified by path/existence only when necessary.

Never persist credentials into target artifacts, logs, tests, state, response,
or documentation.

OUTPUT

Create:

docs/audits/JOB-0009-legacy-historical-artifact-audit.md

The report must contain:

1. scope;
2. legacy data inventory;
3. representative artifacts;
4. provenance chain;
5. digest/integrity findings;
6. correction-layer findings;
7. point-in-time classifications;
8. compatibility with the new fixture pipeline;
9. KRX/Kiwoom historical-data inventory;
10. risks and unknowns;
11. recommended minimal migration/import strategy;
12. recommended next job.

Also create a SMALL machine-readable inventory under an appropriate
research metadata location in the target repository.

Do not copy bulk legacy data.

The machine-readable inventory must reference legacy artifacts by path and
digest rather than duplicate them.

TESTS

Add focused tests for any new inventory/provenance code.

At minimum verify:

- legacy source paths are treated read-only;
- current audit digest is not mislabeled as historical digest;
- correction metadata remains separate from raw source;
- point-in-time classification is explicit;
- missing availability metadata remains unknown;
- representative manifest/digest validation behaves correctly;
- no secret/session content enters generated metadata;
- existing JOB-0006 and JOB-0007 protections continue to pass.

READINESS VERDICT

End with exactly one:

LEGACY_ARTIFACTS_READY_FOR_REAL_FIXTURE
LEGACY_ARTIFACTS_READY_WITH_RESTRICTIONS
LEGACY_ARTIFACTS_REQUIRE_PROVENANCE_REPAIR
LEGACY_ARTIFACTS_NOT_USABLE

This verdict applies only to selecting one real historical fixture.

It does NOT authorize outcome joining or the full three-year study.

QUALITY

Run the complete pytest suite.
Run git diff --check.

Keep implementation minimal.

Do not commit or push.

FINAL RESPONSE

Report:

- requested model tier;
- actual model if telemetry exposes it;
- legacy Telegram date range;
- approximate artifact count;
- representative artifacts audited;
- correction registry found/not found;
- historical digest availability;
- integrity results;
- point-in-time classification counts for audited samples;
- whether existing KRX/Kiwoom historical data was found;
- tests;
- readiness verdict;
- major residual risks;
- recommended next job.

Do not expose credentials or session contents.
